export const synthesis =
  'Chapter 16 organizes APW, KKR, and MTO around one physical fact: the electronic wavefunction has atomic-scale structure near nuclei and much smoother behavior between atoms. APW implements this by augmenting interstitial plane waves with energy-dependent radial solutions and enforcing boundary matching. KKR recasts the same information as single-site scattering embedded in propagation through the lattice, cleanly separating chemistry from geometry and making Green-function descriptions of disorder especially natural. MTOs then reorganize the scattering solution into a compact localized orbital language, from which canonical bands reveal a striking separation between lattice-dependent structure matrices and atom-dependent potential functions. The price of these elegant constructions is energy dependence and nonlinear secular equations. Chapter 17 addresses precisely that difficulty by linearizing the augmented functions to obtain LAPW, LMTO, and related reusable basis sets.';
